WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.178 --> 00:00:22.052
Welcome back to in History—the talk show featuring heavy-hitting interviews with people from the past.

2
00:00:23.133 --> 00:00:24.094
I'm Faye Moss.

3
00:00:25.035 --> 00:00:30.100
Today's guest was a soldier, baseball player, and activist.

4
00:00:30.792 --> 00:00:38.043
He broke the color barrier when he became the first African American player in Major League Baseball.

5
00:00:39.125 --> 00:00:46.115
Straight from history, here is number 42 for the Brooklyn Dodgers, Jackie Robinson!

6
00:01:00.776 --> 00:01:02.237
It's great to be here, Faye.

7
00:01:03.538 --> 00:01:09.341
Before you take us out to the ball game, let's start with your early life.

8
00:01:10.522 --> 00:01:14.384
You were born in Cairo, Georgia, in 1919.

9
00:01:15.605 --> 00:01:16.945
What was your childhood like?

10
00:01:18.186 --> 00:01:22.328
I was the youngest of five kids, and my father left our family when I was a baby.

11
00:01:23.529 --> 00:01:28.432
We then moved to Pasadena, California, where we were the only black family in the neighborhood.

12
00:01:29.808 --> 00:01:33.370
Once a girl called me racist names, so I called her names too.

13
00:01:34.351 --> 00:01:37.152
When her father threw rocks at me, I threw them back!

14
00:01:40.254 --> 00:01:41.735
That must have been hard.

15
00:01:42.675 --> 00:01:45.557
Did being good at sports make things easier?

16
00:01:46.517 --> 00:01:49.579
It did help, because the other kids always wanted me on their team.

17
00:01:50.399 --> 00:01:58.484
In 1939 I went to the University of California, Los Angeles and played four sports: football, basketball, track

18
00:01:58.504 --> 00:02:00.153
and field, and baseball.

19
00:02:01.113 --> 00:02:05.015
Baseball wasn't my best sport; I was only a little interested in it.

20
00:02:05.576 --> 00:02:08.978
Back then I was more interested in a pretty student named Rachel!

21
00:02:10.078 --> 00:02:17.003
You left college before graduating to help your mother, and you played semiprofessional football for a short time.

22
00:02:17.823 --> 00:02:20.064
But then World War II broke out.

23
00:02:21.185 --> 00:02:25.768
I was drafted into the army and spent two years there, but I never saw combat.

24
00:02:26.792 --> 00:02:30.273
I got into some trouble when I refused to move to the back of an army bus.

25
00:02:31.314 --> 00:02:34.935
I knew there were regulations against segregation on military vehicles.

26
00:02:35.975 --> 00:02:38.816
I was accused of insubordination but found innocent.

27
00:02:39.736 --> 00:02:44.298
After I was honorably discharged from the army, I worked as a college basketball coach.

28
00:02:45.718 --> 00:02:50.620
How did you go from coaching college basketball to playing professional baseball?

29
00:02:51.656 --> 00:02:56.438
An army friend who played for the Kansas City Monarchs in the Negro Leagues told me they paid

30
00:02:56.478 --> 00:02:57.019
good money.

31
00:02:58.079 --> 00:03:01.721
Those leagues were formed so that black players could compete in their own league.

32
00:03:02.642 --> 00:03:08.905
I had never dreamed of playing baseball professionally, but there I was accepting an offer from the team.

33
00:03:10.345 --> 00:03:15.408
I enjoyed the pay, but I disliked being away from Rachel, whom I was still dating.

34
00:03:16.412 --> 00:03:21.554
I also didn't like traveling constantly, because black players faced segregation all over the country.

35
00:03:23.594 --> 00:03:29.856
That was the mid-1940s with Jim Crow segregation laws in large parts of the country.

36
00:03:29.876 --> 00:03:31.356
That's right.

37
00:03:32.257 --> 00:03:34.918
And some sports, like boxing, were already integrated.

38
00:03:35.898 --> 00:03:36.858
But not baseball.

39
00:03:37.738 --> 00:03:42.880
The general manager for the Brooklyn Dodgers, Branch Rickey, wanted to break baseball's color barrier.

40
00:03:43.872 --> 00:03:47.333
He was a religious man who thought segregation was morally wrong.

41
00:03:48.294 --> 00:03:52.296
He sent scouts into the Negro Leagues to look for the right man for his team.

42
00:03:53.376 --> 00:03:55.697
I met him in 1945 when I was 26.

43
00:03:57.137 --> 00:03:58.998
What made you the right player?

44
00:04:00.119 --> 00:04:01.719
To be honest, I was a risk.

45
00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:08.722
Mr. Rickey needed a strong athlete who could withstand racist taunts, abuse, and even death threats.

46
00:04:09.903 --> 00:04:14.941
All my life I'd fought back against injustice, but now I'd have to hold back.

47
00:04:15.902 --> 00:04:20.346
Mr. Rickey tested me by yelling terrible things at me to make sure I could handle it.

48
00:04:21.366 --> 00:04:26.871
I realized I couldn't lose my temper, because I'd ruin not just my own chance but also an

49
00:04:26.931 --> 00:04:28.932
opportunity for every black athlete.

50
00:04:30.313 --> 00:04:32.375
After I joined the team, Mr.

51
00:04:32.435 --> 00:04:35.217
Rickey became like the father that I never had.

52
00:04:36.258 --> 00:04:41.902
Rachel and I got married in 1946, and I spent a year in the minors playing in Canada

53
00:04:41.962 --> 00:04:45.345
for the Montreal Royals—one of Brooklyn's affiliate teams.

54
00:04:46.626 --> 00:04:51.250
April 15, 1947 was a truly historic day.

55
00:04:52.131 --> 00:04:59.297
You were called up to play for the Brooklyn Dodgers—the first black player in the Major Leagues!

56
00:05:00.338 --> 00:05:05.823
Yes, but before that season started, some of my teammates had talked about signing a petition to avoid

57
00:05:05.883 --> 00:05:06.524
playing with me.

58
00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:12.143
Across the National League, players threatened to strike, but nothing came of it.

59
00:05:13.144 --> 00:05:19.068
I felt uneasy on Opening Day, standing alone at first base, the only black man on the field.

60
00:05:20.429 --> 00:05:22.150
Was your first season difficult?

61
00:05:23.251 --> 00:05:23.711
It was.

62
00:05:24.932 --> 00:05:27.914
One reporter called me the loneliest man in baseball.

63
00:05:28.955 --> 00:05:32.357
I faced some trouble early on when we played the Philadelphia Phillies.

64
00:05:33.358 --> 00:05:36.260
Their manager called me terrible racist names.

65
00:05:37.952 --> 00:05:40.673
I'd had similar experiences in the minors too.

66
00:05:41.713 --> 00:05:45.914
Pitchers threw at my head, runners kicked me and spiked me with their cleats.

67
00:05:47.094 --> 00:05:51.676
Once a fan threw a black cat on the field, saying, "Hey, Jackie, here's your cousin!"

68
00:05:53.036 --> 00:05:55.077
Rachel supported me through everything.

69
00:05:56.097 --> 00:05:57.537
I couldn't have done it without her.

70
00:05:58.938 --> 00:06:02.338
On the bright side, you were named Rookie of the Year.

71
00:06:02.359 --> 00:06:07.000
And the next year, there were more black players in the MLB.

72
00:06:08.616 --> 00:06:10.517
And that took some pressure off of me!

73
00:06:11.558 --> 00:06:12.599
We had a great season.

74
00:06:13.559 --> 00:06:17.762
The Dodgers were league champions but lost the World Series to the Yankees.

75
00:06:19.843 --> 00:06:22.005
My best year was 1949.

76
00:06:23.126 --> 00:06:27.328
I played in the All-Star Game and was named Most Valuable Player when the season ended.

77
00:06:28.469 --> 00:06:32.512
In 1955 we finally beat the Yankees to win the World Series!

78
00:06:33.576 --> 00:06:38.478
I stole home in the first game, setting my record for home steals at 19.

79
00:06:39.639 --> 00:06:42.420
You went on to play ten seasons for the Dodgers.

80
00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:44.981
What were your career highlights?

81
00:06:45.961 --> 00:06:49.883
When 1956 ended, the Dodgers announced they were going to trade me.

82
00:06:50.943 --> 00:06:52.604
I decided to retire instead.

83
00:06:53.704 --> 00:06:57.326
I ended my career with 972 runs scored, 1,563 hits, and 200 bases stolen.

84
00:07:03.664 --> 00:07:07.407
In 1962 I was inducted into the Baseball Hall of Fame.

85
00:07:08.347 --> 00:07:11.289
Those are impressive statistics.

86
00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:16.112
And you went on to do some impressive activism work too.

87
00:07:17.133 --> 00:07:23.216
You were involved in the Civil Rights Movement and often marched beside Martin Luther King, Jr.

88
00:07:24.137 --> 00:07:28.620
The Civil Rights Movement was important to me, but I also got involved in other causes.

89
00:07:29.684 --> 00:07:33.625
My son, Jackie Jr., fought in Vietnam and returned home a drug addict.

90
00:07:34.485 --> 00:07:39.367
He overcame his addiction and went on to help others, but he died in a car accident at

91
00:07:39.427 --> 00:07:40.327
age 24.

92
00:07:41.347 --> 00:07:44.068
His experience led me to campaign against drugs.

93
00:07:45.288 --> 00:07:50.070
I realized the most important thing I could do was to impact others' lives.

94
00:07:51.230 --> 00:07:52.810
I think you succeeded.

95
00:07:53.711 --> 00:07:55.631
Thank you for joining us, Jackie!

96
00:07:56.731 --> 00:07:58.852
We'll let you get back to history now.

97
00:07:59.776 --> 00:08:00.577
Thank you, Faye.

98
00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:11.007
Nine days after throwing out the first pitch in the 1972 World Series, Jackie Robinson died of a

99
00:08:11.067 --> 00:08:13.309
heart attack at age 53.

100
00:08:14.170 --> 00:08:20.195
His jersey number, 42, was permanently retired in 1997.

101
00:08:21.056 --> 00:08:26.580
Jackie Robinson paved the way for people of color in Major League Baseball and is admired for his

102
00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:29.661
courage and bravery in facing adversity.

103
00:08:30.541 --> 00:08:39.064
In fact each year on April 15, every player, coach, and manager in the MLB wears number 42

104
00:08:39.164 --> 00:08:41.504
to honor Jackie Robinson.

105
00:08:42.425 --> 00:08:45.305
That's all for now on Superstars in History.

106
00:08:45.325 --> 00:08:46.886
I'm Faye Moss.
